  "content": {
    "title": "SpaceX launch recap: Live updates from Starlink mission from Cape Canaveral",
    "text": "5:30 pm ET March 30, 2026Brevard County deactivates launch support after successful SpaceX liftoffRick NealeBrevard County Emergency Management officials have deactivated the agency's launch operations support team after SpaceX’s successful Falcon 9 launch.5:27 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX releases video of booster's 34th landing atop drone shipRick NealeSpaceX officials just tweeted footage of its \"fleet leader booster\" touching down atop the drone ship Just Read the Instructions in the Atlantic Ocean.5:23 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX Falcon 9 booster lands on drone ship Just Read the Instructions in Atlantic OceanRick NealeThe Falcon 9 first-stage booster just landed aboard SpaceX's drone ship Just Read the Instructions in the Atlantic Ocean, completing its 34th mission.5:15 pm ET March 30, 2026Liftoff!Rick NealeLiftoff! SpaceX has just launched the Falcon 9 carrying 29 Starlink satellites from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.5:10 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX Starlink launch coming up in five minutes from Cape CanaveralRick NealeThe countdown continues. Five minutes until liftoff.5:06 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX Starlink rocket launch webcast beginsRick NealeSpaceX's launch webcast is now posted above, right below our countdown clock.Liftoff is scheduled in nine minutes from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.5:02 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX rocket launch coming up from Cape Canaveral, FloridaRick NealeThirteen minutes before SpaceX's Falcon 9 lifts off, the countdown appears to be proceeding as planned.Following is a list of key remaining countdown milestones. T-minus:7 minutes: Falcon 9 begins engine chill prior to launch.1 minute: Command flight computer begins final prelaunch checks; propellant tank pressurization to flight pressure begins.45 seconds: SpaceX launch director verifies “go for launch.”3 seconds: Engine controller commands engine ignition sequence to start.0 seconds: Falcon 9 liftoff.4:55 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX rocket booster to land on drone ship Just Read the InstructionsRick NealeToday's mission will mark the Falcon 9 first-stage booster’s 34th flight, SpaceX reported.The booster previously launched CRS-22, Crew-3, Turksat 5B, Crew-4, CRS-25, Eutelsat HOTBIRD 13G, SES O3B mPOWER-A, PSN SATRIA, Telkomsat Merah Putih 2, Galileo L13, Koreasat-6A and 22 Starlink missions.Following stage separation, crews expect the booster to land on the SpaceX drone ship Just Read the Instructions in the Atlantic Ocean 8 minutes and 21 seconds after liftoff.4:48 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket fueling should be underway at Cape CanaveralRick NealeFalcon 9 fueling procedures should be taking place at Launch Complex 40, SpaceX's ongoing countdown clock indicates.If so, that means the Starlink mission countdown is locked in to lift off at 5:15 p.m. without any delays, or else today's launch must be postponed to a later date.4:33 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX reports anomaly with Starlink satellite, communications lossRick NealeThis morning, SpaceX officials reported that Starlink satellite 34343 experienced an anomaly on Sunday, March 29, about 560 km above the Earth's surface.\"Latest analysis shows the event poses no new risk to the @Space_Station, its crew, or to the upcoming launch of NASA’s Artemis II mission. We will continue to monitor the satellite along with any trackable debris and coordinate with @NASA and the @USSpaceForce,\" a Starlink tweet said.\"The event also posed no new risk to this morning’s Transporter-16 mission, which was designed to avoid Starlink with payload deploys well above or well below the constellation,\" the tweet said.4:21 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX rocket launch prep underway in Brevard CountyRick NealeBrevard County Emergency Management officials have activated the agency's launch operations support team ahead of SpaceX’s upcoming Falcon 9 launch.4:11 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX rocket to launch on heels of California morning missionRick NealeToday's upcoming Florida liftoff will occur hours after another Falcon 9 lifted dozens of payloads into low-Earth orbit from Vandenberg Space Force Base in California.SpaceX's Transporter-16 mission launched at 7:02 a.m. EST.\"Transporter-16 was a dedicated smallsat rideshare mission. There were 119 payloads on this flight, including cubesats, microsats, hosted payloads, a reentry vehicle, and orbital transfer vehicles carrying eight of those payloads to be deployed at a later time,\" the SpaceX website said.\"To date, SpaceX has launched more than 1,600 payloads to orbit across our entire Rideshare program,\" the website said.3:56 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket launch countdown highlightsRick NealeHere's a step-by-step guide to the minutes leading up to launch from SpaceX’s behind-the-scenes Falcon 9 countdown. T-minus:38 minutes: SpaceX launch director verifies “go” for propellant load.35 minutes: Rocket-grade kerosene and first-stage liquid oxygen loading begins.16 minutes: Second-stage liquid oxygen loading begins.7 minutes: Falcon 9 begins engine chill prior to launch.1 minute: Command flight computer begins final prelaunch checks; propellant tank pressurization to flight pressure begins.45 seconds: SpaceX launch director verifies “go” for launch.3 seconds: Engine controller commands engine ignition sequence to start.0 seconds: Liftoff.3:45 pm ET March 30, 2026SpaceX launch to be 22nd of year from Florida’s Space CoastRick NealeSpaceX's upcoming Falcon 9 launch will clock in as the 22nd orbital rocket launch thus far during 2026 from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station and NASA's Kennedy Space Center.Click here for the FLORIDA TODAY Space Team's updating list detailing all of this year’s missions, complete with photo galleries and story links.",
